Run the Extension Host as a worker_thread instead of a forked process, and let a
client reconnect reattach to it.

Completes the pair started with the Pty Host. The Extension Host is the
longest-lived fork the server makes, and on Android 12+ every fork spends one of
32 system-wide background process slots.

It needs more than the Pty Host did, because it hands the client's socket to the
child over IPC and a worker_thread has no channel that can carry a handle.
_canSendSocket is therefore forced off, which selects the named-pipe path VS Code
already uses on Windows: the host connects back over a pipe and the server
bridges the two sockets itself, with no handle ever crossing the IPC boundary.

The pipe path was never written to survive a reconnection, and that is the second
half of this patch. Upstream ends the host's pipe socket when the client's socket
closes, and the host treats that close as a shutdown and terminates. On a desktop
the pipe path is a Windows --socket-path niche; here it is the only path, so every
WebSocket drop killed every extension while the workbench itself looked healthy.
Backgrounding the app for between one and five minutes is enough to reach it: the
WebSocket goes, and the app does not force a reload below five minutes, so the
client reconnects with the same reconnection token onto an extension host that is
already gone.

Splicing the two halves of the old stream back together does not work and is
worth stating, because it is the obvious fix. The server is a byte pipe between
two PersistentProtocol endpoints; when the client's socket dies, whatever the
server had already read from the host and written into it is lost, and neither
endpoint can replay it because neither knows a reconnection happened. Resuming
the same stream onto a new socket hands the client's fresh ProtocolReader the
tail of a message it never saw the head of.

So the host redoes the handshake instead. The named pipe server stays listening
rather than closing after the first connection; the server ends the host's socket
when the client goes and the host dials the pipe again, calling
beginAcceptReconnection / endAcceptReconnection / sendResume as it reattaches.
That restarts its writer at a message boundary, replays what was not
acknowledged, and sends the Resume the client is waiting for after the server's
Pause. The client's own replay covers the other direction, and PersistentProtocol
drops the duplicates. It is upstream's socket-path behaviour reached over a pipe.

Two details that are load-bearing rather than tidy:

The accepted pipe socket is paused whenever no client is attached. A socket with
no 'data' listener is still flowing, so the bytes the host writes between its
re-dial and the client's return would be emitted to nobody and dropped, which
puts the hole back in a place nothing can see.

The grace now lives on the server. Upstream leaves it to the host, which starts
it when its own socket closes; here that close is the reconnect mechanism, so
only the server knows a client is missing. It is capped at
ReconnectionShortGraceTime rather than the three-hour default: an extension host
is the most expensive thing this server holds, the app reloads the page outright
past five minutes in the background, and a renderer crash orphans the connection
under a token the client will never present again.

Where the state machine lands, state by state. Worth writing down because a
desync here presents as extensions silently dying, which is the same symptom as
the bug being fixed, so the next reader has no way to tell them apart from the
outside.

Second and later reconnects. Each cycle ends where it began, so nothing
accumulates: _clientDisconnected clears _clientStore, _pipeStore,
_connectionData and _extHostSocket before it ends the pipe, and the host's next
dial is accepted into a null _extHostSocket. The host keeps one
PersistentProtocol across all of them and calls beginAcceptReconnection per
cycle, which is what upstream's socket path already does per reconnect.

A reconnect after the grace has expired. It cannot arrive: _cleanResources fires
onClose, the agent server deletes the connection, and the token is answered with
"Unknown reconnection token (seen before)". The host learns it the same moment,
from the other side: its socket is destroyed, it dials once more, the pipe server
is already closed, and the failed dial terminates it instead of looping, because
`reconnecting` is cleared only by a successful connect.

A client that never comes back. _scheduleDisconnectTimeout is what disposes the
host, and it is now the ONLY thing that can: the host no longer terminates itself
when its socket closes, so nothing else would reap an orphan. It is armed from
_clientDisconnected, reached from the listeners _watchClient puts on the client's
socket the moment it is stored, in the constructor and in acceptReconnection.
They used to be attached in _pipeSockets, which runs only once the host has
dialled the pipe, and the host's boot is seconds on a phone: a client that
dropped inside that window, a renderer crash or a folder switch right after a
workspace opened, closed a socket nothing was listening to. WebSocketNodeSocket
fires its close once, so the listener attached afterwards never heard it, no
grace was scheduled, and the worker lived until the server exited, one more for
every such drop.

Two clients on one reconnection token. acceptReconnection puts the older one
through the same teardown an observed disconnect gets, then waits for the host's
re-dial. Last one wins, one at a time, which is upstream's semantic as well.

One residual, not introduced here and not closed here:

The pipe stays listening for the life of the connection rather than closing after
the first connect, so anything running as the app UID can dial it during a
reconnect gap and be taken for the extension host. It crosses no boundary that is
not already crossed, since the same UID can read the connection token out of the
app's own files, but the window is wider than upstream's one dial at startup.

MEASURED on an arm64 emulator (API 37) running this tree, both halves.

Under the threshold, the client's socket has to actually go, and backgrounding
alone does not do it there: the app holds a foreground service and never pauses
the WebView, so the page keeps answering and the server logged no disconnect at
all across 150s in the background. SIGSTOP on the app process reaches it, node
being a separate process that keeps running while the client stops answering.
Frozen 120s, past ProtocolConstants.TimeoutTime and inside the grace, then
resumed: the same two connection ids reconnected, the ExtensionHostConnection
logged "The client has reconnected", and the host was the same one, the server's
pid and all three of its worker thread ids unchanged, still unchanged six and a
half minutes later. The grace the teardown prints is 300000ms, the short one.

One line in that log reads alarmingly and is not: acceptReconnection puts the
stale socket through _clientDisconnected, so "will wait 300000ms for a
reconnection before disposing" is printed two milliseconds AFTER "The client has
reconnected". The clearTimeout on the next statement cancels it, which is what
those six and a half minutes confirm. Worth moving the log line the next time
this file is edited, since it is the first thing a reader chasing a disposal
would find.

Past the threshold, 330s in the background: MainActivity forced the reload, the
old connection was disposed gracefully, host <1> exited with code 0, and <2> was
launched two seconds later under fresh connection ids. No "Unknown reconnection
token" page, and the old host's language server and file watcher went with it.

Not measured yet: a client dropped before the host's first dial. That path is
the one change since the runs above, and the grace it now schedules should end
in "The client did not reconnect in time" and a host exit 300s later.

